rust9 — Rust for 9front (self-hosted rustc). Changelog.
(`pac9 changelog rust9` shows this; newest first.)

0.3.0 — 2026-07-26
  cargo9 gets crates.io registry support: `cargo9 install <crate>[@version]`.
  Previously cargo9 was offline-only (local path deps); now it fetches, resolves
  a real dependency graph (feature-closure activation, dev/build/target-cfg
  deps correctly excluded), checksum-verifies (sha256 against the sparse
  index's cksum — the only integrity check, since `hget` gives no TLS chain
  trust), vendors under /usr/glenda/rust/registry/, builds leaves-first, and
  installs the result onto $PATH (mirroring the existing rustc/cargo9 wrapper
  convention). Cleanly refuses (naming the offending crate, not a confusing
  compiler error) anything needing proc-macros, build.rs, or other permanent
  rust9 limits. Verified end-to-end on cirno: `cargo9 install catr` (a real,
  2-hop crates.io dependency chain) builds and runs; `cargo9 install choose`
  (pulls structopt, a proc-macro) and `cargo9 install bitflags@1.2.1` (has a
  build.rs) both refuse cleanly instead of silently misbuilding or ICEing.

  cargo9's own source was lost (a since-deleted session scratchpad) — rewritten
  from scratch at rust9/cargo9/, this time committed. Also fixed along the way,
  all three found live while getting `install` working, none specific to the
  registry feature:
    - std::process::Command spawn/wait was fully broken (ECHILD on every
      child, even ones that ran and exited cleanly) — the plan9 spawn path
      does its own raw rfork, bypassing the registration cc9's own waitpid()
      needs to find the child at all. Fixed in cc9 (new cc9_register_child)
      + the plan9 process backend.
    - `Stdio::from(file)` ("file stdio") was entirely unimplemented on plan9
      (explicit Unsupported error) — now dup2's the file's fd like Stdio::Fd
      already did.
    - A package with BOTH src/lib.rs and src/main.rs (common in real published
      crates, e.g. catr) was previously handled as either/or by the local
      build orchestrator; now lib.rs builds first and links into main.rs.

  Post-implementation code review (before this release) found and fixed
  several real defects, most importantly two path-traversal/command-injection
  holes: an unvalidated crate/bin name from a compromised index response could
  have escaped the vendor directory or embedded shell metacharacters into the
  generated `/bin/rc` wrapper script. Every crate name, version string, and
  bin name is now charset-validated before touching any filesystem path or
  script. Also fixed: the RLIMIT_NPROC fork-bomb counter was silently driven
  negative by every subprocess `install` spawns (missing accounting in the
  new cc9_register_child, not enforcement — a spawn via this path still
  isn't capped up front); `select_latest_nonyanked` could panic on a
  malformed index version; the asm!/build.rs static pre-check never actually
  scanned real source files (wrong directory); `cargo9 build`'s vendored-dep
  lookup ignored the version requirement entirely (naive prefix match, could
  silently link the wrong crate); build artifacts had no killed-mid-compile
  guard (a truncated binary could get "cached" and shipped to $PATH); and a
  `chmod +x`-based install left binaries missing their READ bit under some
  conditions (chmod +x is additive, not absolute — fixed by using chmod 755).
  25 unit tests added (json.rs/toml.rs/registry.rs) covering the parsers, the
  semver/caret matching (including the live `^0` bug), and the validation
  fixes above.

0.2.0 — 2026-07-17
  First versioned release, and the one where the std port stops lying.

  A parity audit of the plan9 std backend found a row of stubs that returned
  a cheerful Ok/false while doing nothing — the exact shape of bug that only
  shows up in production. All fixed, all verified on bare metal:

    - Instant is now MONOTONIC. cc9's clock_gettime only has a realtime clock,
      so Instant could step backwards (timesync, settimeofday) and make
      elapsed() return 0 — hanging every timeout/backoff loop. Now monotonized
      in userspace; it never goes back.
    - File::try_lock / try_lock_shared are HONEST. They used to always "succeed"
      so single-instance guards and cache locks thought they held a lock they
      didn't. Now they return Unsupported instead of corrupting.
    - sync_all / sync_data actually fsync. They used to return Ok(()) doing
      nothing, so a crash after a "synced" commit lost data. Now durable on gefs.
    - Command env no longer LEAKS into the parent. Setting a child's env used to
      mutate the parent's /env (shared env group); now the child gets a private
      group (RFENVG), and env_clear / env_remove really scrub.
    - Network timeouts work: connect_timeout, set_read_timeout, set_write_timeout
      via Plan 9 alarm(2). A dead host returns in the deadline, not in minutes.
    - is_terminal() tracks the real tty (was hardwired false, so colours and
      progress bars never engaged). current_exe() and home_dir() return real
      values instead of Unsupported/None.
    - env::set_var is visible to env::vars() (vars() now reads /env live).

  Under the compiler, the cc9 runtime that your programs link also got the same
  honesty pass: durable fsync, correct O_APPEND, per-thread errno, corrected
  float formatting. Recompile to pick it up.

  Same rustc (1.98.0-dev, cranelift), same n9link, same layout — this is a std +
  runtime correctness release. Earlier unversioned installs read as '-';
  `pac9 upgrade rust9` moves them here.
